{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act grounds based on conviction, delinquency or civil liability","requirement":"A landlord may evict a tenant convicted of assault or terroristic threats against the landlord's family or employees, of theft, or who committed human trafficking, or found civilly liable for such acts or illegal drug activity. Suit within two years; three-day Notice to Quit.","key_value":"3-day Notice to Quit; suit within 2 years","coverage_conditions":"Tenants in residential premises covered by the Eviction Law; the grounds apply where the tenant, or a person the tenant knowingly permits to reside there, was convicted, found delinquent or held civilly liable.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":"The civil-liability ground does not apply to a tenant allowing a juvenile found delinquent for use or possession of drugs to reside at the property.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The landlord may file for eviction, if the tenant has been convicted of or pleaded guilty to, or if a juvenile has been found to be delinquent by the Court due to an offense involving theft of property from the landlord or from tenants residing in the same building or complex.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.78,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Removal of tenants from owner-occupied two- and three-family dwellings","requirement":"A tenant of a landlord-occupied two- or three-family dwelling may be evicted only by court order for holding over, nonpayment of rent, disorderly conduct, wilful damage, constant rule violations, or breach of a lease re-entry clause. Notices: three months, one month or three days.","key_value":"3-month / 1-month notice; 3-day Notice to Quit for cause","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings in New Jersey, whatever the tenancy type (at will, year-to-year, month-to-month or other).","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":3,"owner_dependent":true,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2(a)","quoted_span":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings can be removed only when a court issues an order for eviction.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.78,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rooming and boarding house good-cause and notice rules","requirement":"Owners of rooming and boarding houses must follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, unless the Bureau orders otherwise, and must give the County Welfare Board at least three working days' notice before starting the eviction.","key_value":"3 working days' notice to County Welfare Board","coverage_conditions":"Rooming and boarding houses: buildings with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms, unless a Rooming and Boarding House Act exception applies.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms"},"exemptions":"Hotels with more than 85 percent temporary occupancy by people with homes elsewhere, school and college dormitories, buildings housing only college students, and certain residences for the disabled.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)","quoted_span":"The Regulations Governing Rooming and Boarding Houses, which are enforced by the Bureau of Rooming and Boarding House Standards of the Department of Community Affairs, require owners of rooming and boarding houses to follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, except if otherwise ordered by the Bureau (N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalties for eviction law violations after owner-occupancy or removal notices","requirement":"A landlord who gave notice to personally occupy the unit must occupy it for at least six months, and a landlord who said the building would be permanently retired from residential use must not use the property residentially for five years.","key_value":"6 months' owner occupancy; 5-year residential-use bar","coverage_conditions":"Tenants who vacate a dwelling unit after a notice that the landlord wishes to personally occupy it or that the property is being permanently removed from residential use.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Three times the damages suffered plus attorney fees and costs to the former tenant; civil penalty up to $10,000 per violation; property may not be registered as a planned real estate development for five years.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6","quoted_span":"When a tenant vacates a dwelling unit after having been given notice that the landlord wishes to personally occupy the unit the landlord must occupy the unit for at least six (6) months.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.78,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Reprisal protection for tenants exercising civil rights","requirement":"A landlord may not evict, substantially alter a lease, or refuse to renew a lease in reprisal because the tenant enforced lease or legal rights, made a good faith complaint to a government authority, joined a tenant organization, or refused a retaliatory lease change.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":null},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","penalty":"The tenant may sue the landlord for damages in a civil action.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot take reprisal action against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when a tenant exercises certain civil rights (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.78,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Foreclosure eviction notice protections for tenants","requirement":"After a foreclosure, a landlord must give a tenant a 90-day notice to quit before evicting where the buyer wants to occupy the home; a new owner must notify tenants in English and Spanish within 10 business days of the sale.","key_value":"90-day notice to quit; 10 business days' notice after sale","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of foreclosed property with residential rental units; month-to-month tenants and two- and three-family owner-occupied units are not exempt from the 90-day notice.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":"No exemption stated: foreclosure alone is not grounds for eviction, and the 90-day notice rules reach month-to-month and owner-occupied two- and three-family units.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69","quoted_span":"The 90-day notice may be given 90 days before the lease expires. Month-to-month tenants and two- and three-family owner-occupied units are not exempt from the 90-day notice requirements.","interaction":"Federal law does not preempt State or local law that provides longer time periods or other additional protections for tenants in foreclosure proceedings.","confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act","requirement":"Senior, permanently disabled or qualifying disabled-veteran tenants who lived in a building at least one year before a conversion recording may be protected from eviction for 40 years after the building is converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership.","key_value":"40 years' protection; income not more than 3x county average per person or $50,000, whichever is greater","coverage_conditions":"Tenants at least 62 before the conversion recording, permanently disabled tenants, or honorably discharged disabled veterans (60% or more service-connected), who lived in the building at least one year before the conversion recording date.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Buildings converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership"},"exemptions":"Tenants whose family income exceeds the greater of three times the county average per-person income and $50,000.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28","quoted_span":"Tenants may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three (3) times the average per person income in their county or $50,000.00, whichever is greater (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.78,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant Protection Act of 1992 conversion protections","requirement":"The law extends protected-tenancy protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties, at present only Hudson County, in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible under the Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981.","key_value":"Hudson County only","coverage_conditions":"Qualified tenants in qualified counties, currently only Hudson County, in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible as senior citizens or disabled persons under the 1981 Act.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Conversions in qualified counties (only Hudson County)"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59","quoted_span":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 amendment extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible for Protected Tenancy as either Senior Citizens or Disabled Persons under the “Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981” (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59; N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.1 to -3.4).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rent increase above rent control listed as prohibited landlord conduct","requirement":"The packet lists an increase in rent above any rent control or rent leveling ordinance, or where the property is not rent controlled an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase, among prohibited landlord conduct.","key_value":"No increase above rent control; otherwise no unreasonable or unconscionable increase","coverage_conditions":"Stated in a list of prohibited landlord conduct; the omitted heading and surrounding text do not show which tenancies or properties are covered.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J. Dep't of Community Affairs, Truth in Renting","quoted_span":"An increase in rent in excess of any rent control or rent leveling ordinance, or if the property is not subject to rent control, an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.5,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
